var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"concepts/#Solver-concepts","page":"Solver concepts","text":"This page explains the SNOPT terms that appear in the results, the options and the callback arguments of SNOPT.jl, and the rules that come from the design of the SNOPT library. The SNOPT 7.7 user guide gives the full description.","title":"Solver concepts"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/#Major-and-minor-iterations","page":"Solver concepts","text":"SNOPT uses a sequential quadratic programming (SQP) method. At each major iteration, it replaces the problem near the current point by a quadratic program (QP), in which the objective is quadratic and the constraints are linear, and the solution of this QP gives the search direction for the next point. SNOPT solves each QP with an iterative method whose steps are the minor iterations, so one major iteration usually contains several minor iterations.\n\nSNOPT does not need second derivatives: it builds an approximation of the Hessian of the Lagrangian from the change in the gradients between major iterations. This is the reason why SNOPT.jl asks only for first derivatives. For problems with many variables, the option \"Hessian\" => \"limited memory\" keeps only a few recent updates instead of a full matrix.\n\nThe options \"Major iterations limit\" and \"Iterations limit\" limit the two kinds of iteration, and SnoptResult reports both counts in major_itns and iterations.","title":"Major and minor iterations"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/#Slack-variables","page":"Solver concepts","text":"SNOPT turns each constraint row l_i le c_i(x) le u_i into an equation c_i(x) - s_i = 0 with a bounded slack variable l_i le s_i le u_i. After this change, every inequality is a bound on a variable, and the SNOPT method handles bounds directly. For this reason, several arrays in SNOPT.jl have length n + m, with the n variables first and the m slack variables after them: for example x in SnoptMajorLog and the arrays of the low-level problem types.\n\nSNOPT needs at least one constraint row. When a problem has no constraints, snopt adds one row with infinite bounds, so m = 1 in the arrays above; in the low-level problem types, the caller adds this row.","title":"Slack variables"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/#Basis,-superbasic-variables-and-warm-starts","page":"Solver concepts","text":"At each point, SNOPT divides the variables, including the slack variables, into three groups: nonbasic variables, which are fixed at their lower or upper bound; basic variables, which the linearized constraints determine from the others; and superbasic variables, which are free to move between their bounds. SNOPT changes only the superbasic variables directly, so their number is the dimension of the space in which SNOPT searches for a better point. This division is the basis, and SNOPT records it in an integer state for each variable:\n\nState Meaning\n0 Nonbasic, at the lower bound.\n1 Nonbasic, at the upper bound.\n2 Superbasic.\n3 Basic.\n\nA SnoptBasis stores these states, as hs, together with the number of superbasic variables nS. In a warm start, SNOPT begins with a stored basis instead of constructing a new basis, which saves work when the new problem is close to the old one. A hot start goes further and also reuses the LU factorization of the basis matrix and the Hessian approximation inside the SNOPT workspace; Warm and hot starts shows how to use both.","title":"Basis, superbasic variables and warm starts"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/#Infinite-bounds","page":"Solver concepts","text":"SNOPT treats any bound with absolute value of at least 10^20 as infinite. The function snopt converts Inf and -Inf to 1.0e20 and -1.0e20, so either form works there. The low-level problem types pass the arrays to SNOPT without change and check only their lengths, so they need ±1.0e20 for an infinite bound. snopt rejects a NaN bound; the low-level types do not check for it.","title":"Infinite bounds"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/#Status-codes","page":"Solver concepts","text":"When a solve ends, SNOPT returns an integer status code. SNOPT.jl stores it in result.status as a value of the enum SnoptStatus, whose integer value is the code itself: Int(result.status) gives the code, and SnoptStatus.T(code) gives the value. Program logic tests the value, for example result.status == SnoptStatus.Optimal; a misspelt name then fails with an UndefVarError instead of comparing as false. The tens digit of a code is its group, so Int(result.status) ÷ 10 == 1 holds for every infeasible outcome. The SNOPT 7.7 user guide explains each code.\n\nCodes SnoptStatus values Meaning\n1 Optimal Optimal solution found.\n2 FeasiblePoint Feasible point found; the objective was not optimized (option \"Feasible point only\").\n3 AccuracyNotAchieved The requested accuracy could not be achieved.\n5–6 ElasticObjectiveMinimized, ElasticInfeasibilitiesMinimized Results of SNOPT's elastic mode, which relaxes the constraints marked as elastic.\n11–16 InfeasibleLinear, InfeasibleLinearEqualities, NonlinearInfeasibilitiesMinimized, LinearInfeasibilitiesMinimized, InfeasibleQPSubproblem, InfeasibleNonelastic The constraints cannot be satisfied.\n21–22 UnboundedObjective, ConstraintViolationLimit The objective decreases without limit, or the constraint violation limit was reached.\n31–32 IterationLimit, MajorIterationLimit The minor or the major iteration limit was reached.\n33 SuperbasicsLimit The option \"Superbasics limit\" is too small.\n34 TimeLimit The CPU time limit (option \"Time limit\") was reached.\n41–45 CannotImprove, SingularBasis, CannotSatisfyConstraints, IllConditionedNullSpace, LUFactorizationFailed SNOPT could not continue for numerical reasons.\n51, 52, 56 IncorrectObjectiveDerivatives, IncorrectConstraintDerivatives, BadlyScaledFunctions The derivatives appear to be wrong, or the functions are badly scaled.\n61–63 UndefinedAtFirstFeasiblePoint, UndefinedAtInitialPoint, UndefinedRegion A function could not be evaluated at a point.\n71–74 StopInFunction, StopInConstraints, StopInObjective, StopInMonitor A user function returned false.\n81–84 WorkArraysTooSmall, CharacterStorage, IntegerStorage, RealStorage The SNOPT workspace is too small.\n91–92 InvalidInput, BasisFileMismatch The problem data or the basis file are not valid.\n141–142 WrongBasicVariableCount, BasisPackageError Error inside SNOPT.\nany other Unknown (0) A code that the SNOPT 7.7 user guide does not list.","title":"Status codes"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/#One-SNOPT-workspace-per-Julia-process","page":"Solver concepts","text":"The SNOPT library allows only one active session in each process. SNOPT.jl calls this session, together with its integer and real work arrays, the workspace. SNOPT.jl therefore runs solves one at a time: when several tasks or threads call snopt at the same time, each call waits until the previous call has finished. To solve several problems in parallel, start several Julia processes, for example with the Distributed standard library, because each process loads its own copy of the library.\n\nWith the Low-level interface, the caller creates the workspace with initialize. Each call to initialize, snopt or snmemb waits for a running solve to end, then opens a new workspace and closes the workspace that was open before. For this reason, none of them may be called while a low-level workspace is still in use. Manage a low-level workspace from one task only. Do not call any SNOPT.jl function that uses the workspace from inside a callback function while a solve is running: such a call throws an ArgumentError.","title":"One SNOPT workspace per Julia process"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/#Limits","page":"Solver concepts","text":"snopt needs exact first derivatives. To let SNOPT estimate derivatives by finite differences, use SnoptA.\nsnopt treats all constraints as nonlinear. To give linear constraints separately, use SnoptA.\nsnopt does not support hot starts and does not read specs files.\nThe variables and the bounds are converted to Float64.\nIteration counts and solve times are correct only with SNOPT 7.7.","title":"Limits"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/options/#Set-solver-options","page":"Set solver options","text":"SNOPT has more than 100 options, such as tolerances, iteration limits and print levels. Each option has a name of one or more words, for example \"Major iterations limit\". The SNOPT option reference gives all names, default values and meanings.","title":"Set solver options"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/options/#Pass-options-to-snopt","page":"Set solver options","text":"Give options as a vector of pairs name => value:\n\nresult = snopt(objective, gradient!, x0;\n               options = [\"Major iterations limit\" => 500,\n                          \"Major optimality tolerance\" => 1.0e-8,\n                          \"Hessian\" => \"limited memory\"])\n\nPart Allowed values\nName A String, or a Symbol in which _ stands for a space.\nValue An integer, a finite floating-point number, a String (passed unchanged) or a Symbol (_ stands for a space).\n\nThus :major_iterations_limit => 500 is the same as \"Major iterations limit\" => 500, and :hessian => :limited_memory is the same as \"Hessian\" => \"limited memory\". SNOPT does not distinguish upper case and lower case in option names.\n\nSNOPT.jl rejects true and false as values, because Julia would pass them to SNOPT as the integers 1 and 0. Use the integer that the SNOPT option reference gives.\n\nAn option name that SNOPT does not know, or a value that SNOPT does not accept, throws an ArgumentError before the solve starts.","title":"Pass options to snopt"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/options/#Frequently-used-options","page":"Set solver options","text":"Option Effect\n\"Major iterations limit\" Maximum number of major iterations.\n\"Iterations limit\" Maximum total number of minor iterations.\n\"Major optimality tolerance\" Tolerance on the optimality conditions.\n\"Major feasibility tolerance\" Tolerance on the constraint violation.\n\"Time limit\" Maximum CPU time of the solve, in seconds.\n\"Hessian\" \"full memory\" or \"limited memory\". Use limited memory for many variables.\n\"Verify level\" 0 to 3: check the derivatives against finite differences. -1 turns the check off.\n\"Major print level\" 0 writes no iteration table to the log file.\n\nSolver concepts explains major and minor iterations.","title":"Frequently used options"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/options/#Write-the-SNOPT-log-to-a-file","page":"Set solver options","text":"By default, SNOPT.jl keeps no SNOPT output. Use these keywords of snopt to keep it:\n\nKeyword Default Content\nprintfile \"\" Path of the print file, the full log with one line per iteration. \"\" writes no file.\nsummfile \"\" Path of the summary file, a short log of the main events. \"\" writes no file.\nname \"Julia\" Problem name that SNOPT writes in the log. A name longer than 8 bytes throws an ArgumentError.\n\nresult = snopt(objective, gradient!, x0;\n               printfile = \"solve.out\",\n               options = [\"Major print level\" => 1])\n\nWhen both paths are \"\", SNOPT.jl creates a temporary summary file, because SNOPT needs one file to write to, and deletes it when the workspace closes. The temporary directory must therefore be writable; otherwise, pass summfile.","title":"Write the SNOPT log to a file"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/options/#Read-options-from-a-specs-file","page":"Set solver options","text":"A specs file is a text file that contains SNOPT options, one option on each line, between the lines Begin and End:\n\nBegin\n   Major iterations limit       500\n   Major optimality tolerance   1.0e-8\nEnd\n\nsnopt does not read specs files. With the Low-level interface, call read_options on an open workspace:\n\ninitialize(\"\", \"\") do workspace\n    read_options(workspace, \"problem.spc\")\n    # Build and solve a low-level problem with this workspace.\nend\n\nread_options returns 101 when it reads the file without errors. specs_status_message explains the other codes.","title":"Read options from a specs file"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/lowlevel/#Low-level-interface","page":"Low-level interface","text":"The low-level interface gives direct access to SNOPT's three solver interfaces, snOptA, snOptB and snOptC. SNOPT.jl calls them through the C functions of snopt-interface. The function snopt is a simpler front end to snOptB; the low-level interface is necessary for these features:\n\nFinite-difference derivatives.\nLinear constraints, given separately from the nonlinear constraints.\nHot starts.\nOptions read from a specs file.\n\nWith the low-level interface, the caller creates the SNOPT workspace, builds the problem arrays in the form that each SNOPT interface expects, and reads the results from the problem object. SNOPT.jl checks the array lengths, but it does not add the slack variables. It passes the bounds to SNOPT without change and does not check them for NaN: use ±1.0e20, not ±Inf, for an infinite bound.\n\nProblem type SNOPT interface User functions Use for\nSnoptA snOptA One function for all rows; derivatives optional Linear rows, finite differences\nSnoptB snOptB Separate objective and constraint functions Control of the workspace and the start mode\nSnoptC snOptC One function for objective and constraints Shared work between objective and constraints\n\nsnopt! solves any of the three types. snopta!, snoptb! and snoptc! solve one type each.","title":"Low-level interface"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/lowlevel/#Workspace","page":"Low-level interface","text":"A workspace is the SNOPT session and its integer and real work arrays. Create it with initialize. The do form closes the workspace at the end of the block, also after an error:\n\nusing SNOPT\n\ninitialize(\"\", \"\") do workspace\n    set_option!(workspace, \"Major print level\", 0)\n    # Build and solve problems with this workspace.\nend\n\nThe two string arguments are the paths of the print file and the summary file; \"\" writes no file. If you do not use the do form, call close(workspace) when you are done.\n\nOnly one workspace can be open in a Julia process, and initialize closes the previous workspace. Solver concepts explains this limit.","title":"Workspace"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/lowlevel/#Workspace-size","page":"Low-level interface","text":"The default workspace has 30500 integer and 60000 real entries. A larger problem needs more. snmemb asks SNOPT for the minimum sizes of an snOptB or snOptC problem:\n\nmemory = snmemb(m, n, neJ, negCon, nnCon, nnObj, nnJac)\ninitialize(\"\", \"\", memory.miniw, memory.minrw) do workspace\n    # ...\nend\n\nArgument Meaning\nm Number of constraint rows (at least 1).\nn Number of variables.\nneJ Number of entries in the Jacobian sparsity pattern.\nnegCon Number of those entries that belong to nonlinear constraints.\nnnCon Number of nonlinear constraints.\nnnObj Number of variables that appear nonlinearly in the objective.\nnnJac Number of variables that appear nonlinearly in the constraints.\n\nSNOPT requires that the nonlinear variables come first in x: the objective is nonlinear in x[1:nnObj] only, and the constraints in x[1:nnJac] only. Each work array must have at least 500 entries. For an SnoptA problem, there is no sizing function: if the solve ends with a status from 81 to 84, open a larger workspace.","title":"Workspace size"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/lowlevel/#Options","page":"Low-level interface","text":"set_option! sets one option on an open workspace:\n\nset_option!(workspace, \"Major iterations limit\", 250)\nset_option!(workspace, \"Major optimality tolerance\", 1.0e-8)\nset_option!(workspace, \"Hessian limited memory\")\n\nThe option names are the same as in Set solver options. read_options reads a specs file, as that page also describes.","title":"Options"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/lowlevel/#SnoptA:-finite-differences,-linear-rows-and-hot-starts","page":"Low-level interface","text":"SnoptA puts the objective and all constraints in one vector F(x) of nf rows. Row objrow is the objective; the other rows are constraints with bounds flow ≤ F ≤ fupp. The derivatives of F come in two parts, both as lists of (row, column) positions:\n\nThe linear part: constant entries A[k] at position (iAfun[k], jAvar[k]).\nThe nonlinear part: positions (iGfun[k], jGvar[k]) whose values the user function computes.\n\nThis example minimizes (x_1 - 2)^2 without derivatives, so SNOPT estimates them by finite differences. It then changes the starting point and solves again with a hot start.\n\njulia> using SNOPT\n\njulia> initialize(\"\", \"\") do workspace\n           set_option!(workspace, \"Derivative option\", 0)  # finite differences\n           usrfun = make_usrfun_a((F, x) -> (F[1] = (x[1] - 2.0)^2))\n           problem = SnoptA(workspace,\n                            1, 1,                     # nf, n\n                            0.0, 1,                   # objadd, objrow\n                            Int32[], Int32[], Float64[],   # iAfun, jAvar, A: no linear part\n                            Int32[1], Int32[1],       # iGfun, jGvar: F[1] depends on x[1]\n                            [-10.0], [10.0],          # xlow, xupp\n                            [-1.0e20], [1.0e20],      # flow, fupp: objective row free\n                            [0.0], zeros(Int32, 1), zeros(1),   # x, xstate, xmul\n                            zeros(1), zeros(Int32, 1), zeros(1), # F, Fstate, Fmul\n                            0, 0, 0, 0.0,             # status, nS, num_inf, sum_inf\n                            usrfun)\n           status1 = snopta!(problem)\n           problem.x[1] = 2.1                         # new starting point\n           status2 = snopta!(problem; start = \"Hot\")\n           (status1, status2, round(problem.x[1]; digits = 4))\n       end\n(1, 1, 2.0)\n\nsnopta! returns the SNOPT status code (here 1, optimal solution found). It writes the solution into problem.x, the row values into problem.F, and the multipliers into problem.xmul and problem.Fmul.\n\nThe option \"Derivative option\" tells SNOPT which derivatives the user function gives: 1, the default, means all of them, and 0 means that SNOPT estimates the missing ones. To give derivatives, pass eval_G to make_usrfun_a: eval_G(G, x) writes the value at position (iGfun[k], jGvar[k]) into G[k].","title":"SnoptA: finite differences, linear rows and hot starts"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/lowlevel/#SnoptB:-separate-objective-and-constraint-functions","page":"Low-level interface","text":"SnoptB is the type that snopt builds internally. All its constraints are nonlinear, and SNOPT.jl takes the constraints as nonlinear in all n variables (nnJac = n). The arrays x, bl, bu and hs have length n + m_eff: the n variables first, then one slack variable for each of the m_eff constraint rows. A problem without constraints still needs one row with infinite bounds, so m_eff = 1, and this row must be included in the arrays. Its Jacobian pattern J is a 1 × n matrix with one entry, because SNOPT needs at least one. J must have the type SparseMatrixCSC{Float64, Int32}; sparse gives this type when the index vectors are Int32.\n\njulia> using SNOPT\n\njulia> using SparseArrays\n\njulia> initialize(\"\", \"\") do workspace\n           n, m_eff = 2, 1\n           objective(x) = (x[1] - 1.0)^2 + (x[2] - 2.0)^2\n           function gradient!(g, x)\n               g[1] = 2.0 * (x[1] - 1.0)\n               g[2] = 2.0 * (x[2] - 2.0)\n               return nothing\n           end\n           objfun = make_objfun(objective, gradient!, workspace.iw)\n           confun = make_dummy_confun()                   # no constraints\n           J = sparse(Int32[1], Int32[1], [0.0], m_eff, n)  # one entry at (1, 1)\n           problem = SnoptB(workspace,\n                            n, 0, m_eff, n,              # n, nc, m_eff, nnobj\n                            zeros(n + m_eff),            # x\n                            [-10.0, -10.0, -1.0e20],     # bl\n                            [10.0, 10.0, 1.0e20],        # bu\n                            zeros(Int32, n + m_eff),     # hs\n                            J,\n                            0.0, 0, Float64[],           # obj_val, status, lambda\n                            objfun, confun)\n           status = snoptb!(problem)\n           (status, round.(problem.x[1:n]; digits = 4))\n       end\n(1, [1.0, 2.0])\n\nAfter the solve, problem.x, problem.obj_val, problem.lambda and problem.hs contain the result.","title":"SnoptB: separate objective and constraint functions"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/lowlevel/#SnoptC:-one-function-for-objective-and-constraints","page":"Low-level interface","text":"SnoptC has the same arrays as SnoptB, but SNOPT calls one function for the objective and the constraints. make_usrfun_c builds this function from the same four Julia functions that snopt uses. This example minimizes (x_1 - 2)^2 subject to x_1 ge 1:\n\njulia> using SNOPT\n\njulia> using SparseArrays\n\njulia> initialize(\"\", \"\") do workspace\n           objective(x) = (x[1] - 2.0)^2\n           gradient!(g, x) = (g[1] = 2.0 * (x[1] - 2.0))\n           constraints!(c, x) = (c[1] = x[1])\n           jacobian!(vals, x) = (vals[1] = 1.0)\n           J = sparse(Int32[1], Int32[1], [1.0], 1, 1)\n           usrfun = make_usrfun_c(objective, gradient!, constraints!, jacobian!, J,\n                                  workspace.iw)\n           problem = SnoptC(workspace,\n                            1, 1, 1, 1,                  # n, nc, m_eff, nnobj\n                            [0.0, 0.0],                  # x\n                            [-10.0, 1.0],                # bl: variable, then constraint\n                            [10.0, 1.0e20],              # bu\n                            zeros(Int32, 2),             # hs\n                            J,\n                            0.0, 0, Float64[],           # obj_val, status, lambda\n                            usrfun)\n           status = snoptc!(problem)\n           (status, round(problem.x[1]; digits = 4))\n       end\n(1, 2.0)","title":"SnoptC: one function for objective and constraints"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/lowlevel/#Callback-builders","page":"Low-level interface","text":"SNOPT calls C functions with a fixed argument list. The builders wrap Julia functions in functions of that form:\n\nBuilder User functions Used by\nmake_objfun eval_obj(x), eval_grad(g, x) SnoptB\nmake_confun eval_con(c, x), eval_jac(vals, x) SnoptB\nmake_dummy_confun none (no constraints) SnoptB\nmake_usrfun_c all four of the above SnoptC\nmake_usrfun_a eval_F(F, x), optional eval_G(G, x) SnoptA\n\nThe user functions follow the rules for callback functions. Each builder accepts the keyword callback, which is called after each evaluation and stops the solve if it returns false, as in Monitor and stop a solve. The fields of its NamedTuple argument depend on the builder; the docstring of each builder lists them. To pass snlog or snstop, give them as keywords to the solve function, for example snoptb!(problem; snlog = f).","title":"Callback builders"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/monitoring/#Monitor-and-stop-a-solve","page":"Monitor and stop a solve","text":"snopt accepts three optional functions that SNOPT.jl calls during the solve. Each function receives one argument that describes the current state. If a function returns false, SNOPT stops the solve; any other return value lets the solve continue.\n\nKeyword Called Argument\nsnlog Once per major iteration SnoptMajorLog\nsnstop Once per major iteration SnoptStopEvent\ncallback After each evaluation of the objective or the constraints NamedTuple\n\nUse snlog to print or record progress. Use snstop for a stopping rule that needs gradients or multipliers. Use callback only when you need every function evaluation: SNOPT evaluates the functions several times per major iteration, so this function is called much more often.","title":"Monitor and stop a solve"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/monitoring/#Print-progress","page":"Monitor and stop a solve","text":"result = snopt(objective, gradient!, x0;\n               snlog = function (event)\n                   println(\"iteration \", event.major_iter,\n                           \": f = \", event.objective,\n                           \", infeasibility = \", event.primal_infeasibility)\n                   return true\n               end)\n\nThe most useful fields of SnoptMajorLog are:\n\nField Content\nmajor_iter Number of the current major iteration.\nobjective Current objective value.\nprimal_infeasibility Constraint violation, scaled.\ndual_infeasibility Error in the optimality conditions, scaled.\nx Current variables, followed by one slack variable per constraint.\n\nThe docstring of SnoptMajorLog lists all fields. SNOPT can scale the variables and the constraints to improve the numerical behaviour (option \"Scale option\"), and it computes the two infeasibility values for the scaled problem. They decrease to the tolerances in Set solver options as the solve converges.","title":"Print progress"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/monitoring/#Stop-on-a-custom-condition","page":"Monitor and stop a solve","text":"This example stops the solve after 30 seconds of wall-clock time:\n\ndeadline = time() + 30.0\nresult = snopt(objective, gradient!, x0;\n               snstop = event -> time() < deadline)\nresult.status   # SnoptStatus.StopInMonitor if the deadline was reached\n\nWhen a function returns false, the result has a status from SnoptStatus.StopInFunction to SnoptStatus.StopInMonitor (codes 71–74), and result.x is the point at which SNOPT stopped. To limit only the number of iterations or the CPU time, use the options \"Major iterations limit\" or \"Time limit\" instead. The option \"Time limit\" measures CPU time, so use snstop to stop on wall-clock time, as in this example.\n\nSnoptStopEvent has all the fields of SnoptMajorLog, and also the objective gradient gobj, the Jacobian values gcon, the multipliers pi and the scaled bounds bl and bu.","title":"Stop on a custom condition"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/monitoring/#Observe-each-function-evaluation","page":"Monitor and stop a solve","text":"For snopt, the callback function receives a NamedTuple with these fields. The callback builders of the Low-level interface use other kind values and fields; their docstrings list them.\n\nField Content\nkind :objective or :constraint.\nmode 0 if SNOPT asked for the value only, 2 if it also asked for derivatives.\nmajor_iter, minor_iter Always 0 in this version. Use snlog for iteration counts.\nx Copy of the point.\nf Objective value. Only when kind == :objective.\nc Copy of the constraint values. Only when kind == :constraint.\n\nBefore SNOPT starts, SNOPT.jl evaluates each function once at x0, with each entry clamped to its bounds [lb, ub]. This finds errors in the user functions early. callback is also called for these first evaluations.\n\nIf callback returns false during these first evaluations, SNOPT does not start. The result then has the status SnoptStatus.StopInFunction, and result.basis contains no information: a warm start from it is the same as a cold start.","title":"Observe each function evaluation"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#Getting-started","page":"Getting started","text":"In this tutorial, we solve two small problems with snopt, the simplest entry point of SNOPT.jl. The first problem has only variable bounds; the second adds nonlinear constraints and a sparse Jacobian. Both examples need a working SNOPT library (see Installation), and the second also uses the SparseArrays standard library.","title":"Getting started"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#Solve-a-problem-with-bounds","page":"Getting started","text":"We want to minimize f(x) = (x_1 - 1)^2 + (x_2 - 2)^2 subject to -10 le x_i le 10. The solution is x = (1 2) with f(x) = 0.\n\nSNOPT needs two functions: one that returns the objective value, and one that writes the gradient into an array that SNOPT supplies. We define both and call snopt:\n\njulia> using SNOPT\n\njulia> objective(x) = (x[1] - 1.0)^2 + (x[2] - 2.0)^2;\n\njulia> function gradient!(g, x)\n           g[1] = 2.0 * (x[1] - 1.0)\n           g[2] = 2.0 * (x[2] - 2.0)\n           return nothing\n       end;\n\njulia> x0 = [0.0, 0.0];\n\njulia> result = snopt(objective, gradient!, x0; lb = -10.0, ub = 10.0);\n\njulia> result.status\nSnoptStatus.Optimal = 1\n\nThe arguments of this call are:\n\nobjective(x) returns the objective value as a real number.\ngradient!(g, x) writes partial f  partial x_i into g[i] for every i. SNOPT ignores its return value.\nx0 is the starting point. Its length sets the number of variables.\nlb and ub are the variable bounds. A scalar applies to every variable; a vector gives one bound per variable. An omitted bound is infinite.\n\nBy default, snopt prints nothing and keeps no log file. To change tolerances or limits, or to keep the SNOPT log, see Set solver options.","title":"Solve a problem with bounds"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#Read-the-result","page":"Getting started","text":"snopt returns a SnoptResult. We check the status first, because result.x is meaningful only after a successful solve:\n\njulia> result.status == SnoptStatus.Optimal || error(\"SNOPT failed: $(result.status)\");\n\njulia> round.(result.x; digits = 6)\n2-element Vector{Float64}:\n 1.0\n 2.0\n\nField Content\nstatus Outcome of the solve, a SnoptStatus value such as SnoptStatus.Optimal. Int(result.status) is the SNOPT status code.\nx Solution.\nobjective Objective value at x.\nlambda Lagrange multipliers: one per variable, then one per constraint.\nmajor_itns Number of major iterations.\n\nSolver concepts lists all status codes. The SnoptResult docstring lists all fields.","title":"Read the result"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#Add-nonlinear-constraints","page":"Getting started","text":"Next, we solve problem 71 of the Hock–Schittkowski test set, which has four variables and two nonlinear constraints:\n\nbeginaligned\nmin_x quad  x_1 x_4 (x_1 + x_2 + x_3) + x_3 \ntextsubject to quad  x_1 x_2 x_3 x_4 ge 25 \n x_1^2 + x_2^2 + x_3^2 + x_4^2 = 40 \n 1 le x_i le 5 quad i = 1 dots 4\nendaligned\n\nThe optimal objective value is approximately 17014.\n\nA constrained problem needs four more keyword arguments:\n\nKeyword Content\neval_con eval_con(c, x) writes the constraint values into c.\neval_jac eval_jac(vals, x) writes the Jacobian entries into vals.\nlcon Lower constraint bounds. Use -Inf for no lower bound.\nucon Upper constraint bounds. Use Inf for no upper bound.\n\nEqual lower and upper bounds give an equality constraint. Here the first constraint has lcon = 25 and ucon = Inf, and the second has lcon = ucon = 40.\n\nThe Jacobian is the matrix J_ij = partial c_i  partial x_j. We pass its sparsity pattern, the positions of the entries that can be nonzero, as a sparse matrix J; SNOPT.jl ignores the values stored in J. The function eval_jac must write the entries in the order of J.nzval, which for a Julia SparseMatrixCSC is column by column.\n\njulia> using SNOPT\n\njulia> using SparseArrays\n\njulia> objective(x) = x[1] * x[4] * (x[1] + x[2] + x[3]) + x[3];\n\njulia> function gradient!(g, x)\n           g[1] = x[4] * (2.0 * x[1] + x[2] + x[3])\n           g[2] = x[1] * x[4]\n           g[3] = x[1] * x[4] + 1.0\n           g[4] = x[1] * (x[1] + x[2] + x[3])\n           return nothing\n       end;\n\njulia> function constraints!(c, x)\n           c[1] = x[1] * x[2] * x[3] * x[4]\n           c[2] = x[1]^2 + x[2]^2 + x[3]^2 + x[4]^2\n           return nothing\n       end;\n\njulia> # Every entry of this 2×4 Jacobian can be nonzero.\n       J = sparse([1, 2, 1, 2, 1, 2, 1, 2], [1, 1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 4, 4], ones(8), 2, 4);\n\njulia> # Column order: (1,1), (2,1), (1,2), (2,2), (1,3), (2,3), (1,4), (2,4).\n       function jacobian!(vals, x)\n           vals[1] = x[2] * x[3] * x[4]\n           vals[2] = 2.0 * x[1]\n           vals[3] = x[1] * x[3] * x[4]\n           vals[4] = 2.0 * x[2]\n           vals[5] = x[1] * x[2] * x[4]\n           vals[6] = 2.0 * x[3]\n           vals[7] = x[1] * x[2] * x[3]\n           vals[8] = 2.0 * x[4]\n           return nothing\n       end;\n\njulia> result = snopt(objective, gradient!, [1.0, 5.0, 5.0, 1.0];\n                      lb = 1.0, ub = 5.0,\n                      eval_con = constraints!, eval_jac = jacobian!,\n                      lcon = [25.0, 40.0], ucon = [Inf, 40.0], J = J);\n\njulia> (result.status, round(result.objective; digits = 3))\n(SnoptStatus.Optimal, 17.014)\n\nWithout J, SNOPT.jl assumes that every Jacobian entry can be nonzero and orders the entries column by column, as in this example. For large problems, pass J: SNOPT is designed for sparse Jacobians, and a dense pattern makes each iteration slower.\n\nwarning: Include every entry that can become nonzero\nThe sparsity pattern is fixed for the whole solve. Include an entry if it can be nonzero at any point, even if it is zero at x0. An entry that is missing from J is taken as zero, and SNOPT then works with a wrong Jacobian.","title":"Add nonlinear constraints"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#Rules-for-the-callback-functions","page":"Getting started","text":"These rules apply to gradient!, eval_con and eval_jac:\n\nWrite every entry of the output array on every call.\nDo not change x.\nDo not keep a reference to x or to the output array after the function returns. SNOPT reuses their memory, so copy them to keep their values.\nAvoid memory allocation, because SNOPT calls these functions many times.\n\nAn error thrown inside a callback stops the solve. snopt closes its SNOPT workspace and then throws the error again to the caller.","title":"Rules for the callback functions"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#Next-steps","page":"Getting started","text":"Control tolerances, limits and output files: Set solver options.\nPrint progress or add a custom stopping rule: Monitor and stop a solve.\nSolve a sequence of related problems faster: Warm and hot starts.\nLet SNOPT estimate derivatives, or give linear constraints separately: Low-level interface.\n\nThe repository contains both examples as scripts in examples/. Run them from the repository root:\n\njulia --project=. examples/unconstrained.jl\njulia --project=. examples/hs71.jl\n\nEach script writes the SNOPT log to a .out file next to the script.","title":"Next steps"},{"category":"section","location":"api/monitoring/#Monitoring-API","page":"Monitoring API","text":"Pages = [\"monitoring.md\"]","title":"Monitoring API"},{"category":"type","location":"api/monitoring/#SNOPT.SnoptMajorLog","page":"Monitoring API","text":"SnoptMajorLog\n\nState of the solve at the end of a major iteration, passed to snlog. Return false to stop the solve. The arrays are copies; most values refer to SNOPT's scaled problem. See Monitor and stop a solve.\n\nFields\n\niteration, major_iter, minor_iter: total minor iterations, current major iteration, and minor iterations in it.\nn_superbasics, n_swaps: number of superbasic variables and of basis changes.\nobjective: objective value, including objective_add and a linear objective row.\nmerit: merit function, the objective plus a penalty on the constraint violation.\nf_objective, f_merit: nonlinear parts of the objective and of the merit function.\nprimal_infeasibility, dual_infeasibility: constraint violation and error in the optimality conditions.\nmax_violation, relative_violation: largest constraint violation, absolute and relative.\nstep, penalty_norm: length of the last step and norm of the penalty parameters.\ncondition_hessian: estimated condition number of the reduced Hessian.\nobjective_scale, objective_add: scale factor and constant of the objective.\nminimize: 1 to minimize, -1 to maximize.\nkt_conditions: two flags, true when the primal and the dual Karush–Kuhn–Tucker conditions hold.\nn, nb, nncon, nnobj: number of variables, of variables plus slack variables, of nonlinear constraints and of nonlinear objective variables.\nx, hs: variables followed by slack variables, and their basis states; length nb.\nfcon, fx, ycon: nonlinear constraint values as SNOPT passes them (fCon and Fx), and their multipliers; length nncon.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SNOPT.SnoptMajorLog"},{"category":"type","location":"api/monitoring/#SNOPT.SnoptStopEvent","page":"Monitoring API","text":"SnoptStopEvent\n\nState of the solve at the end of one major iteration, passed to the snstop function.\n\nThis type has all the fields of SnoptMajorLog and the fields below. Return false from snstop to stop the solve; the result then has the status SnoptStatus.StopInMonitor. See Monitor and stop a solve.\n\nFields\n\nm: number of constraint rows.\nmax_superbasics: largest allowed number of superbasic variables.\nnegcon: number of nonlinear Jacobian entries.\ngobj: objective gradient, length nnobj.\ngcon: nonlinear Jacobian entries, length negcon.\npi: multipliers of the rows, length m.\nrc: reduced costs of the variables and slack variables, length nb.\nrg: reduced gradient, length max_superbasics.\nbl, bu: scaled lower and upper bounds, length nb.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SNOPT.SnoptStopEvent"},{"category":"function","location":"api/monitoring/#SNOPT.snopt_no_progress","page":"Monitoring API","text":"snopt_no_progress(event) -> true\n\nReturn true for every event, so the solve never stops.\n\nPass it as callback, snlog or snstop where a function that never stops the solve is needed. The default value nothing of these keywords has the same effect and is faster, because SNOPT.jl then does not create the event.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SNOPT.snopt_no_progress"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/warm_start/#Warm-and-hot-starts","page":"Warm and hot starts","text":"When you solve a sequence of related problems, for example the same model with slightly different parameters, you can start each solve from the end state of the previous one. SNOPT then often needs fewer iterations. SNOPT has three start modes:\n\nMode Starts from Available in\nCold The point x0 only. This is the default. snopt and the low-level interface\nWarm x0 and the basis of a previous solve. snopt and the low-level interface\nHot x0, the basis, the basis factorization and the Hessian approximation of a previous solve. Low-level interface only\n\nThe basis records which variables and constraints were at a bound at the end of a solve. Solver concepts explains it in more detail.","title":"Warm and hot starts"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/warm_start/#Warm-start-with-snopt","page":"Warm and hot starts","text":"Pass the basis of the previous SnoptResult and start = \"Warm\". Usually you also start from the previous solution:\n\nlb, ub = -10.0, 10.0\nresult1 = snopt(objective, gradient!, x0; lb = lb, ub = ub)\n\nresult2 = snopt(objective, gradient!, result1.x; lb = lb, ub = ub,\n                start = \"Warm\", basis = result1.basis)\n\nThe two problems must have the same number of variables and the same number of constraints. The functions and the bounds can be different.\n\nsnopt throws an ArgumentError in these cases:\n\nstart = \"Warm\" without basis.\nbasis without start = \"Warm\".\nA basis from a problem with a different size.\n\nIf a solve stopped during the first evaluations, before SNOPT started (see Monitor and stop a solve), its basis contains no information. A warm start from it is then the same as a cold start.","title":"Warm start with snopt"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/warm_start/#Hot-start","page":"Warm and hot starts","text":"A hot start also reuses the LU factorization of the basis matrix and the Hessian approximation that SNOPT keeps in its workspace. These exist only in the workspace of the previous solve. snopt creates a new workspace for each call, so it rejects start = \"Hot\".\n\nTo use a hot start:\n\nOpen one workspace with initialize and build a low-level problem.\nSolve the problem.\nChange values in the same problem object, for example the starting point x. Do not change the sizes or the sparsity pattern.\nSolve again with start = \"Hot\", before you close the workspace.\n\nThe SnoptA example in the low-level interface does these steps.","title":"Hot start"},{"category":"section","location":"api/highlevel/#High-level-API","page":"High-level API","text":"Pages = [\"highlevel.md\"]","title":"High-level API"},{"category":"section","location":"api/highlevel/#Solve-a-problem","page":"High-level API","text":"","title":"Solve a problem"},{"category":"section","location":"api/highlevel/#Status","page":"High-level API","text":"","title":"Status"},{"category":"section","location":"api/highlevel/#Library-setup","page":"High-level API","text":"These functions are not exported. Call them as SNOPT.has_snopt() and SNOPT.find_snopt_lib().","title":"Library setup"},{"category":"function","location":"api/highlevel/#SNOPT.snopt","page":"High-level API","text":"snopt(eval_obj, eval_grad, x0; lb = nothing, ub = nothing,\n      eval_con = nothing, eval_jac = nothing, lcon = nothing, ucon = nothing,\n      J = nothing, options = nothing, callback = nothing, snlog = nothing,\n      snstop = nothing, printfile = \"\", summfile = \"\", start = \"Cold\",\n      basis = nothing, name = \"Julia\") -> SnoptResult\n\nMinimize eval_obj(x) subject to variable bounds and optional nonlinear constraints, and return a SnoptResult. x0 and the bound arrays are not changed. See Getting started.\n\nArguments\n\neval_obj(x): return the objective value.\neval_grad(g, x): write every entry of the objective gradient into g.\nx0: starting point, nonempty and finite, of length n.\n\nKeywords\n\nlb, ub: variable bounds, scalars or vectors of length n. nothing and ±Inf mean no bound.\neval_con(c, x), eval_jac(vals, x): write the m constraint values into c and the Jacobian entries into vals, in the order of J.nzval.\nlcon, ucon: constraint bounds of length m; equal values give an equality. Give them together with eval_con and eval_jac.\nJ: sparsity pattern of the m × n Jacobian, a SparseMatrixCSC; nothing means dense.\noptions: vector of name => value pairs; see Set solver options.\ncallback, snlog, snstop: monitoring functions; a return value of false stops the solve. See Monitor and stop a solve.\nstart, basis: \"Warm\" with the SnoptBasis of an earlier result starts from that basis. See Warm and hot starts.\nprintfile, summfile: paths of the SNOPT log and summary; \"\" writes no file.\nname: problem name in the SNOPT log, at most 8 bytes.\n\nThrows an ArgumentError for invalid input, such as a NaN bound, crossed bounds, a wrong array length, an unknown option or start = \"Hot\". An error in a user function is thrown again after the workspace is closed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SNOPT.snopt"},{"category":"type","location":"api/highlevel/#SNOPT.SnoptResult","page":"High-level API","text":"SnoptResult\n\nResult of snopt.\n\nCheck status before using x, for example result.status == SnoptStatus.Optimal.\n\nFields\n\nstatus: outcome of the solve, a SnoptStatus value; Int(status) is the SNOPT status code.\nobjective: objective value at x.\nx: solution, length n.\nlambda: Lagrange multipliers, one per variable and then one per constraint.\nnum_inf: number of bounds and constraints that are still violated.\nsum_inf: sum of these violations.\niterations: total number of minor iterations.\nmajor_itns: number of major iterations.\nrun_time: CPU time of the solve reported by SNOPT, in seconds.\nmemory: SnoptMemory used to size the workspace.\nbasis: SnoptBasis for a warm start.\n\nIf a callback function stopped the solve before SNOPT started, basis contains no information and the iteration counts are zero.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SNOPT.SnoptResult"},{"category":"type","location":"api/highlevel/#SNOPT.SnoptBasis","page":"High-level API","text":"SnoptBasis\n\nBasis at the end of a solve, for a warm start with snopt.\n\nPass result.basis as the basis keyword of a later call, together with start = \"Warm\". See Warm and hot starts and Solver concepts.\n\nFields\n\nhs: basis state of each variable and slack variable (Int32), length n + m.\nnS: number of superbasic variables.\nn: number of variables.\nm: number of constraint rows; 1 for a problem without constraints.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SNOPT.SnoptBasis"},{"category":"module","location":"api/highlevel/#SNOPT.SnoptStatus","page":"High-level API","text":"SnoptStatus\n\nStatus of a SNOPT solve. Each value of type SnoptStatus.T is a SNOPT 7.7 status code: SnoptStatus.T(51) gives SnoptStatus.IncorrectObjectiveDerivatives, and Int gives the code back. The tens digit is the group, so Int(status) ÷ 10 == 1 holds for every infeasible outcome. SnoptStatus.Unknown (0) stands for a code that the SNOPT 7.7 user guide does not list. Solver concepts describes each group.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> SnoptStatus.T(51)\nSnoptStatus.IncorrectObjectiveDerivatives = 51\n\njulia> Int(SnoptStatus.TimeLimit)\n34\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SNOPT.SnoptStatus"},{"category":"function","location":"api/highlevel/#SNOPT.status_from_code","page":"High-level API","text":"SNOPT.status_from_code(code::Integer) -> SnoptStatus.T\n\nReturn the SnoptStatus value of the SNOPT status code code, or SnoptStatus.Unknown for a code that the SNOPT 7.7 user guide does not list. Unlike SnoptStatus.T(code), it never throws, so it is safe to call on the code returned by snopt!, snopta!, snoptb! or snoptc!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SNOPT.status_from_code"},{"category":"function","location":"api/highlevel/#SNOPT.has_snopt","page":"High-level API","text":"has_snopt() -> Bool\n\nReturn true if SNOPT.jl found a usable SNOPT library when the package was loaded.\n\nIf this is false, each solve throws an error. Use SNOPT.find_snopt_lib to find the cause, and see Troubleshooting.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SNOPT.has_snopt"},{"category":"function","location":"api/highlevel/#SNOPT.find_snopt_lib","page":"High-level API","text":"find_snopt_lib() -> String\n\nReturn the absolute path of a usable libsnopt7, or \"\" if none is found.\n\nA library is usable if it loads and has every C function that SNOPT.jl calls. The search checks SNOPTDIR, then LD_LIBRARY_PATH, DYLD_LIBRARY_PATH (macOS) or PATH (Windows), then the default locations of the system loader. It warns if SNOPTDIR is set but holds no usable library.\n\nSNOPT.jl runs this search once at load time and stores the result in SNOPT.libsnopt7; a later call does not change it, so restart Julia after fixing the setup. See also SNOPT.has_snopt.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SNOPT.find_snopt_lib"},{"category":"section","location":"installation/#Installation","page":"Installation","text":"SNOPT.jl needs two parts: the Julia package, installed with the package manager, and the SNOPT shared library, available from the SNOPT distributors. This page explains how to connect the two and how to check the result.","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"installation/#Requirements","page":"Installation","text":"Julia 1.11 or later.\nA licensed SNOPT 7.7 shared library, libsnopt7, that includes the C interface from snopt-interface.\n\nSNOPT.jl reads the iteration counts and the solve time from fixed positions in the SNOPT 7.7 workspace. With another SNOPT version, a solve can be correct while these values are wrong.","title":"Requirements"},{"category":"section","location":"installation/#Install-the-package","page":"Installation","text":"using Pkg\nPkg.add(\"SNOPT\")\n\nTo use the development version:\n\nusing Pkg\nPkg.develop(url = \"https://github.com/EllissoideRotondo/SNOPT.jl\")\n\nThe package loads without the SNOPT library. In that case SNOPT.has_snopt returns false, and each solve throws an error that explains how to make the library available.","title":"Install the package"},{"category":"section","location":"installation/#Make-the-library-available","page":"Installation","text":"The library file has a different name on each platform:\n\nPlatform File name\nLinux libsnopt7.so\nmacOS (Intel) libsnopt7.dylib\nWindows libsnopt7.dll\n\nSet the environment variable SNOPTDIR to the directory that contains this file. Do this before you start Julia.\n\nLinux and macOS (bash or zsh):\n\nexport SNOPTDIR=/path/to/snopt/lib\n\nWindows (PowerShell):\n\n$env:SNOPTDIR = \"C:\\path\\to\\snopt\\lib\"\n\nSNOPT.jl looks for the library once, when you load the package. If you change SNOPTDIR later, restart Julia.\n\nSome SNOPT licenses also need the variable SNOPT_LICENSE. Follow the instructions that come with your SNOPT distribution.","title":"Make the library available"},{"category":"section","location":"installation/#Search-order","page":"Installation","text":"SNOPT.jl accepts the first library that it can load and that contains all the C functions it needs. It looks in these places, in this order:\n\nThe directory in SNOPTDIR. If SNOPTDIR is set but the library there cannot be used, SNOPT.jl shows a warning and continues.\nThe directories in LD_LIBRARY_PATH (Linux and macOS), DYLD_LIBRARY_PATH (macOS) or PATH (Windows).\nThe default locations of the system loader, for example /usr/lib on Linux.\n\nIf a file libiomp5 (the Intel OpenMP runtime library) is in the same directory as libsnopt7, SNOPT.jl also loads it. If that fails, SNOPT.jl continues without it.","title":"Search order"},{"category":"section","location":"installation/#Check-the-setup","page":"Installation","text":"This command prints the path of the library that SNOPT.jl found. If you installed SNOPT.jl in a project environment, add --project=/path/to/project after julia.\n\njulia -e 'using SNOPT; @assert SNOPT.has_snopt(); println(SNOPT.libsnopt7)'\n\nIn PowerShell:\n\njulia -e \"using SNOPT; @assert SNOPT.has_snopt(); println(SNOPT.libsnopt7)\"\n\nThen solve the first problem in Getting started.","title":"Check the setup"},{"category":"section","location":"installation/#Platform-support","page":"Installation","text":"Platform Status\nLinux Supported and tested.\nWindows Supported with a MinGW build of libsnopt7.dll.\nmacOS (Intel) Expected to work. Not tested.\nmacOS (Apple silicon) Not supported.","title":"Platform support"},{"category":"section","location":"installation/#Troubleshooting","page":"Installation","text":"Start with the values that SNOPT.jl sees:\n\nusing SNOPT\n\nSNOPT.has_snopt()        # true if a usable library was found\nSNOPT.libsnopt7          # path of that library, or \"\"\nSNOPT.find_snopt_lib()   # repeat the search now\n\nSNOPT.find_snopt_lib does not change SNOPT.libsnopt7. After you correct the setup, restart Julia.","title":"Troubleshooting"},{"category":"section","location":"installation/#has_snopt()-returns-false","page":"Installation","text":"Make sure that SNOPTDIR is the directory that contains the library, not the path of the library file.\nMake sure that your user can read the file.\nIf find_snopt_lib() also returns \"\", no directory in the search order contains a usable library.","title":"has_snopt() returns false"},{"category":"section","location":"installation/#The-library-exists-but-does-not-load","page":"Installation","text":"The system loader cannot load the library, or the library does not contain the C interface. On Linux, ldd /path/to/libsnopt7.so lists the shared libraries that are missing.\n\nSNOPT.jl needs the C functions from snopt-interface, for example f_sninitx, f_snoptb and f_snkera. SNOPT.jl checks for all of them before it accepts a library. A library built only from the Fortran source does not contain these functions. Use a SNOPT distribution that includes the C interface, or build SNOPT again with snopt-interface.","title":"The library exists but does not load"},{"category":"section","location":"installation/#Windows","page":"Installation","text":"SNOPT.jl needs a libsnopt7.dll built with MinGW, the GNU compiler for Windows. The library built with the Intel compiler for Visual Studio passes arguments in a different way and does not work with SNOPT.jl. If you only have a Linux library, use the Windows Subsystem for Linux (WSL).","title":"Windows"},{"category":"section","location":"#SNOPT.jl:-Julia-interface-to-the-SNOPT-solver","page":"Home","text":"SNOPT.jl is an unofficial Julia interface to SNOPT, a commercial solver for large, sparse, constrained nonlinear optimization problems. The package wraps all three SNOPT solver interfaces, snOptA, snOptB and snOptC, and so gives access to the SNOPT features, including linear constraints, finite-difference derivatives, warm and hot starts, and specs files. For most problems, the function snopt is the simplest entry point: it takes the objective, the constraints and their first derivatives as ordinary Julia functions, manages the SNOPT workspace, and returns the solution in a SnoptResult.\n\nSNOPT.jl solves problems of the form\n\nbeginaligned\nmin_x in mathbbR^n quad  f(x) \ntextsubject to quad  l_x le x le u_x \n l_c le c(x) le u_c\nendaligned\n\nwhere f is the objective, c is a vector of constraint functions, and the bounds can be infinite. SNOPT uses a sequential quadratic programming (SQP) method, so f and c must be smooth, and their first derivatives must be supplied.\n\nnote: SNOPT license required\nSNOPT.jl does not include SNOPT. A SNOPT license and a compatible libsnopt7 shared library are available from the SNOPT distributors. See Installation.","title":"SNOPT.jl: Julia interface to the SNOPT solver"},{"category":"section","location":"#When-to-use-SNOPT.jl","page":"Home","text":"SNOPT.jl is the right choice for calling SNOPT directly with derivatives written by hand. Use OptimizationSNOPT.jl instead to define the problem with Optimization.jl, compute derivatives with automatic differentiation, or compare SNOPT with other solvers through one interface. OptimizationSNOPT.jl uses SNOPT.jl internally.","title":"When to use SNOPT.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Installation","page":"Home","text":"Install the package with the Julia package manager:\n\nusing Pkg\nPkg.add(\"SNOPT\")\n\nThen, before starting Julia, set SNOPTDIR to the directory of the SNOPT library:\n\nexport SNOPTDIR=/path/to/snopt/lib\n\nInstallation gives the library file names, the search order, the commands for Windows and the setup checks.","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"#Contents","page":"Home","text":"","title":"Contents"},{"category":"section","location":"#Tutorial-and-installation","page":"Home","text":"The tutorial solves a first problem with bounds and nonlinear constraints. The installation page makes the SNOPT library available to Julia.\n\nPages = [\n    \"getting_started.md\",\n    \"installation.md\",\n]\nDepth = 2","title":"Tutorial and installation"},{"category":"section","location":"#How-to-guides","page":"Home","text":"Each guide shows how to do one task with SNOPT.jl.\n\nPages = [\n    \"howto/options.md\",\n    \"howto/monitoring.md\",\n    \"howto/warm_start.md\",\n    \"howto/lowlevel.md\",\n]\nDepth = 2","title":"How-to guides"},{"category":"section","location":"#Concepts-and-reference","page":"Home","text":"The concepts page explains the SNOPT terms and status codes. The API reference lists every exported function and type.\n\nPages = [\n    \"concepts.md\",\n    \"api/highlevel.md\",\n    \"api/monitoring.md\",\n    \"api/lowlevel.md\",\n]\nDepth = 2","title":"Concepts and reference"},{"category":"section","location":"#Contributing","page":"Home","text":"Report bugs and request features on the issue tracker.\nBefore opening a pull request, run the test suite with julia --project=. -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.test()' from the repository root. The solver tests run only when Julia finds a licensed libsnopt7.","title":"Contributing"},{"category":"section","location":"#Acknowledgements","page":"Home","text":"SNOPT was written by Philip E. Gill, Walter Murray and Michael A. Saunders. SNOPT.jl draws on earlier Julia wrappers: snopt/SNOPT7.jl, byuflowlab/Snopt.jl and Yuricst/joptimise.","title":"Acknowledgements"},{"category":"section","location":"api/lowlevel/#Low-level-API","page":"Low-level API","text":"Pages = [\"lowlevel.md\"]","title":"Low-level API"},{"category":"section","location":"api/lowlevel/#Workspace-and-options","page":"Low-level API","text":"","title":"Workspace and options"},{"category":"section","location":"api/lowlevel/#Problem-types","page":"Low-level API","text":"","title":"Problem types"},{"category":"section","location":"api/lowlevel/#Solve-functions","page":"Low-level API","text":"","title":"Solve functions"},{"category":"section","location":"api/lowlevel/#Callback-builders","page":"Low-level API","text":"","title":"Callback builders"},{"category":"function","location":"api/lowlevel/#SNOPT.initialize","page":"Low-level API","text":"initialize(printfile, summfile, leniw = 30500, lenrw = 60000) -> SnoptWorkspace\ninitialize(f, printfile, summfile, leniw = 30500, lenrw = 60000)\n\nOpen a SNOPT workspace with integer and real work arrays of length leniw and lenrw (at least 500), and return it. printfile and summfile are the paths of the SNOPT log and summary; \"\" writes no file. snmemb gives the sizes for a large problem.\n\nThe second form calls f(workspace), closes the workspace, also on error, and returns the value of f. Close a workspace from the first form with close.\n\nOnly one workspace is open per Julia process, so initialize closes the previous one; do not call it from a callback. Throws an error if the SNOPT library is not loaded.\n\nExamples\n\ninitialize(\"\", \"\") do workspace\n    set_option!(workspace, \"Major print level\", 0)\n    # Build and solve a low-level problem.\nend\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SNOPT.initialize"},{"category":"type","location":"api/lowlevel/#SNOPT.SnoptWorkspace","page":"Low-level API","text":"SnoptWorkspace\n\nSNOPT session with its integer and real work arrays.\n\nCreate a workspace with initialize, and close it with close or with the do form of initialize. isopen(workspace) tells if it is still open. Only one workspace can be open in a Julia process: initialize closes the previous one. snopt manages its own workspace, so this type is needed only for the Low-level interface.\n\nThe only public field is iw, the integer work array, which the callback builders make_objfun, make_confun and make_usrfun_c take as an argument.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SNOPT.SnoptWorkspace"},{"category":"function","location":"api/lowlevel/#SNOPT.set_option!","page":"Low-level API","text":"set_option!(ws, option::String) -> Int\nset_option!(ws, name::String, value::Real) -> Int\n\nSet one SNOPT option in the workspace ws and return 0.\n\nws is an open SNOPT.SnoptWorkspace or a low-level problem. The first form takes the full option text, for example \"Hessian limited memory\". The second form takes the option name and a number, for example set_option!(ws, \"Major iterations limit\", 500).\n\nThrows an ArgumentError if SNOPT does not accept the option, if value is a Bool or not finite, or if the workspace is closed. To set options in snopt, use its options keyword instead; see Set solver options.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SNOPT.set_option!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/lowlevel/#SNOPT.read_options","page":"Low-level API","text":"read_options(ws, specsfile::String) -> Int\n\nRead SNOPT options from the specs file specsfile into the workspace ws, and return the SNOPT status code.\n\nws is an open SNOPT.SnoptWorkspace or a low-level problem. The status code is 101 if SNOPT read the file without errors; otherwise SNOPT.jl shows a warning, and specs_status_message explains the code. Throws an ArgumentError if the file does not exist. See Set solver options for the file format.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SNOPT.read_options"},{"category":"function","location":"api/lowlevel/#SNOPT.specs_status_message","page":"Low-level API","text":"specs_status_message(info::Int) -> String\n\nReturn a description of the status code info from read_options.\n\n101 means that SNOPT read the specs file without errors. A code info > 134 means that SNOPT found info - 134 errors in the file.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SNOPT.specs_status_message"},{"category":"function","location":"api/lowlevel/#SNOPT.snmemb","page":"Low-level API","text":"snmemb(m, n, neJ, negCon, nnCon, nnObj, nnJac; options = nothing,\n       printfile = \"\", summfile = \"\") -> SnoptMemory\n\nReturn the minimum workspace sizes that SNOPT needs for an snOptB or snOptC problem.\n\nThe arguments are the problem dimensions: m constraint rows (at least 1), n variables, neJ Jacobian entries, negCon nonlinear Jacobian entries, nnCon nonlinear constraints, nnObj nonlinear objective variables and nnJac nonlinear Jacobian variables. Some options change the sizes, so pass the options that the solve will use.\n\nsnmemb opens and closes a temporary workspace, so it closes any open workspace. Throws an ArgumentError if the dimensions are not consistent.\n\nExamples\n\nmemory = snmemb(m, n, neJ, negCon, nnCon, nnObj, nnJac)\ninitialize(\"\", \"\", memory.miniw, memory.minrw) do workspace\n    # Build and solve the problem.\nend\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SNOPT.snmemb"},{"category":"type","location":"api/lowlevel/#SNOPT.SnoptMemory","page":"Low-level API","text":"SnoptMemory\n\nMinimum workspace sizes returned by snmemb.\n\nFields\n\ninfo: SNOPT status code of the estimate. 100 and 104 mean success.\nminiw: minimum length of the integer work array.\nminrw: minimum length of the real work array.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SNOPT.SnoptMemory"},{"category":"type","location":"api/lowlevel/#SNOPT.AbstractSnoptProblem","page":"Low-level API","text":"AbstractSnoptProblem\n\nSupertype of the low-level problem types SnoptA, SnoptB and SnoptC.\n\nEach problem holds a SNOPT.SnoptWorkspace, the problem data and the user functions. snopt! solves any subtype in place.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SNOPT.AbstractSnoptProblem"},{"category":"type","location":"api/lowlevel/#SNOPT.SnoptA","page":"Low-level API","text":"SnoptA(ws, nf, n, objadd, objrow, iAfun, jAvar, A, iGfun, jGvar,\n       xlow, xupp, flow, fupp, x, xstate, xmul, F, Fstate, Fmul,\n       status, nS, num_inf, sum_inf, usrfun)\n\nLow-level problem for SNOPT's snOptA interface; solve it with snopta!. The positional arguments are the fields below. See Low-level interface.\n\nThe objective and the constraints form one vector F(x) of nf rows, with the objective in row objrow. The derivatives of F are split into constant entries and entries that usrfun computes.\n\nFields\n\nws: open SNOPT.SnoptWorkspace.\nnf, n: number of rows of F and number of variables.\nobjadd, objrow: constant added to the objective, and its row (0 for none).\niAfun, jAvar, A: rows, columns (Int32) and values of the constant entries.\niGfun, jGvar: rows and columns (Int32) of the entries that usrfun computes.\nxlow, xupp, flow, fupp: bounds of x (length n) and of F (length nf); ±1.0e20 means no bound.\nx, xstate, xmul: variables, basis states (Int32) and multipliers, length n. x holds the starting point, then the solution.\nF, Fstate, Fmul: the same for the rows, length nf.\nstatus, nS, num_inf, sum_inf: outputs; pass 0, 0, 0, 0.0.\nusrfun: function from make_usrfun_a.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SNOPT.SnoptA"},{"category":"type","location":"api/lowlevel/#SNOPT.SnoptB","page":"Low-level API","text":"SnoptB(ws, n, nc, m_eff, nnobj, x, bl, bu, hs, J, obj_val, status, lambda,\n       objfun, confun, nS = 0)\n\nLow-level problem for SNOPT's snOptB interface, with separate objective and constraint functions; solve it with snoptb!. The positional arguments are the fields below. See Low-level interface.\n\nFields\n\nws: open SNOPT.SnoptWorkspace.\nn, nc: number of variables and of constraints; all constraints are nonlinear.\nm_eff: number of rows, max(nc, 1); SNOPT needs at least one.\nnnobj: the objective is nonlinear in x[1:nnobj]; usually n.\nx, bl, bu, hs: values, bounds and basis states (Int32) of the variables followed by one slack variable per row, length n + m_eff. ±1.0e20 means no bound; hs is zero for a cold start.\nJ: m_eff × n sparsity pattern, a SparseMatrixCSC{Float64, Int32} with at least one entry.\nobj_val, status, lambda: outputs; pass 0.0, 0, Float64[].\nobjfun, confun: from make_objfun, and from make_confun or make_dummy_confun.\nnS: number of superbasic variables; 0 for a cold start.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SNOPT.SnoptB"},{"category":"type","location":"api/lowlevel/#SNOPT.SnoptProblem","page":"Low-level API","text":"SnoptProblem\n\nAlias for SnoptB.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SNOPT.SnoptProblem"},{"category":"type","location":"api/lowlevel/#SNOPT.SnoptC","page":"Low-level API","text":"SnoptC(ws, n, nc, m_eff, nnobj, x, bl, bu, hs, J, obj_val, status, lambda,\n       usrfun, nS = 0)\n\nLow-level problem for SNOPT's snOptC interface, with one function for the objective and the constraints. Solve it with snoptc!.\n\nThe fields are the same as those of SnoptB, except that one function usrfun replaces objfun and confun. Build usrfun with make_usrfun_c. See Low-level interface for an example.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SNOPT.SnoptC"},{"category":"function","location":"api/lowlevel/#SNOPT.snopt!","page":"Low-level API","text":"snopt!(prob::AbstractSnoptProblem; start = \"Cold\", name = \"Julia\",\n       snlog = nothing, snstop = nothing) -> Int\n\nSolve the low-level problem prob in place with snopta!, snoptb! or snoptc!, and return the SNOPT status code.\n\nstart is \"Cold\", \"Warm\" (from the basis in prob) or \"Hot\" (also reuses the factorization and Hessian of the previous solve of prob in the same workspace). snlog, snstop and name work as in snopt.\n\nThrows an ArgumentError if the workspace is closed, a solve is running, or start or name is not valid. An error in a user function is thrown again after the solve.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SNOPT.snopt!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/lowlevel/#SNOPT.snopta!","page":"Low-level API","text":"snopta!(prob::SnoptA; start = \"Cold\", name = \"Julia\", snlog = nothing,\n        snstop = nothing) -> Int\n\nSolve prob in place with SNOPT's snOptA interface and return the SNOPT status code.\n\nAfter the solve, prob.x, prob.F, prob.xmul, prob.Fmul, prob.xstate, prob.Fstate and prob.status hold the result. The keywords are the same as for snopt!. If prob.usrfun gives no derivatives, set the option \"Derivative option\" to 0 before the solve.\n\nThrows a DimensionMismatch if the array lengths in prob do not agree.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SNOPT.snopta!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/lowlevel/#SNOPT.snoptb!","page":"Low-level API","text":"snoptb!(prob::SnoptB; start = \"Cold\", name = \"Julia\", snlog = nothing,\n        snstop = nothing) -> Int\n\nSolve prob in place with SNOPT's snOptB interface and return the SNOPT status code.\n\nAfter the solve, prob.x, prob.obj_val, prob.lambda, prob.hs, prob.nS and prob.status hold the result. The keywords are the same as for snopt!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SNOPT.snoptb!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/lowlevel/#SNOPT.snoptc!","page":"Low-level API","text":"snoptc!(prob::SnoptC; start = \"Cold\", name = \"Julia\", snlog = nothing,\n        snstop = nothing) -> Int\n\nSolve prob in place with SNOPT's snOptC interface and return the SNOPT status code.\n\nAfter the solve, prob.x, prob.obj_val, prob.lambda, prob.hs, prob.nS and prob.status hold the result. The keywords are the same as for snopt!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SNOPT.snoptc!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/lowlevel/#SNOPT.make_usrfun_a","page":"Low-level API","text":"make_usrfun_a(eval_F; eval_G = nothing, callback = nothing)\n\nWrap eval_F(F, x) and eval_G(G, x) in the user function of a SnoptA problem.\n\neval_F(F, x) writes every row value into F. eval_G(G, x) writes the derivative at position (iGfun[k], jGvar[k]) into G[k]. If eval_G is nothing, SNOPT estimates the derivatives by finite differences; then set the option \"Derivative option\" to 0.\n\nIf callback is a function, it is called after each evaluation of F with a NamedTuple with the fields kind = :function, x and F. If it returns false, SNOPT stops.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SNOPT.make_usrfun_a"},{"category":"function","location":"api/lowlevel/#SNOPT.make_objfun","page":"Low-level API","text":"make_objfun(eval_obj, eval_grad, ws_iw; callback = nothing)\n\nWrap eval_obj(x) and eval_grad(g, x) in the objective function of a SnoptB problem.\n\neval_obj(x) returns the objective value; eval_grad(g, x) writes every entry of the gradient into g. Pass the integer work array workspace.iw as ws_iw.\n\nIf callback is a function, it is called after each objective evaluation with a NamedTuple with the fields kind = :objective, mode, major_iter, minor_iter, x and f. If it returns false, SNOPT stops. During a solve, major_iter and minor_iter are 0; use snlog for iteration counts.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SNOPT.make_objfun"},{"category":"function","location":"api/lowlevel/#SNOPT.make_confun","page":"Low-level API","text":"make_confun(eval_con, eval_jac, J, ws_iw; callback = nothing)\nmake_confun(eval_con, eval_jac, J)\n\nWrap eval_con(c, x) and eval_jac(vals, x) in the constraint function of a SnoptB problem.\n\neval_con(c, x) writes every constraint value into c. eval_jac(vals, x) writes the Jacobian entries into vals, in the order of J.nzval. Pass the integer work array workspace.iw as ws_iw.\n\nIf callback is a function, it is called after each constraint evaluation with a NamedTuple with the fields kind = :constraint, mode, major_iter, minor_iter, x and c. If it returns false, SNOPT stops. During a solve, major_iter and minor_iter are 0; use snlog for iteration counts.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SNOPT.make_confun"},{"category":"function","location":"api/lowlevel/#SNOPT.make_dummy_confun","page":"Low-level API","text":"make_dummy_confun()\n\nReturn a constraint function that does nothing, for a SnoptB problem without constraints.\n\nSNOPT needs a constraint function even when there are no nonlinear constraints.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SNOPT.make_dummy_confun"},{"category":"function","location":"api/lowlevel/#SNOPT.make_usrfun_c","page":"Low-level API","text":"make_usrfun_c(eval_obj, eval_grad, eval_con, eval_jac, J, ws_iw; callback = nothing)\nmake_usrfun_c(eval_obj, eval_grad, eval_con, eval_jac, J)\n\nWrap the objective and constraint functions in the user function of a SnoptC problem.\n\nThe four functions have the same form as in snopt: eval_obj(x) returns the objective, eval_grad(g, x) writes the gradient, eval_con(c, x) writes the constraint values, and eval_jac(vals, x) writes the Jacobian entries in the order of J.nzval. Pass workspace.iw as ws_iw.\n\nIf callback is a function, it is called after each evaluation with a NamedTuple with the fields kind = :combined, mode, major_iter, minor_iter, x, f and c. If it returns false, SNOPT stops. During a solve, major_iter and minor_iter are 0; use snlog for iteration counts.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SNOPT.make_usrfun_c"},{"category":"function","location":"api/lowlevel/#SNOPT.make_snlog","page":"Low-level API","text":"make_snlog(callback)\n\nWrap callback in a function that SNOPT can call once per major iteration.\n\nSNOPT.jl calls callback with a SnoptMajorLog. If it returns false, SNOPT stops. The solve functions call make_snlog when they receive the snlog keyword, so a direct call is rarely necessary.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SNOPT.make_snlog"},{"category":"function","location":"api/lowlevel/#SNOPT.make_snstop","page":"Low-level API","text":"make_snstop(callback)\n\nWrap callback in a function that SNOPT can call once per major iteration.\n\nSNOPT.jl calls callback with a SnoptStopEvent. If it returns false, SNOPT stops with status code 74 (SnoptStatus.StopInMonitor). The solve functions call make_snstop when they receive the snstop keyword, so a direct call is rarely necessary.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SNOPT.make_snstop"}]
}
